  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon continues to feature slight \nridging over the western half of the CONUS that terminates to the \neast with a shortwave-powered MCS that is currently moving \nthrough the Great Lakes Region. Zooming in locally, a somewhat \nmessy pattern is in place with a swath of leftover stratus \ncreating a differential heating boundary while just to the west,\nan area of scattered clouds with greater vertical extent push \neastward towards it. Observations have been coming in rather \nsoup-ey across far southeast Nebraska into southwest Iowa, where\ndewpoints in the low-to-mid 70s alongside quickly-climbing \ntemperatures have led to heat indices in the upper 80s and 90s \nalready. Lower dewpoints are being advected in from the \nsouthwest and could curb how high apparent temperatures reach, \nespecially for southern Nebraska. A Heat Advisory is in place \nfor Pawnee/Richardson/Nemaha Counties through 8 PM, where the \nhighest temperatures are expected to occur. There is some chance\nthat an expansion is needed to the north, particularly into \nsouthwest Iowa.\n\nOther than the heat, we'll be on the lookout for some very minor \nrain chances as forcing for ascent intersects a boundary laid across \nsouthern Nebraska this evening into the overnight hours.Chances \nincrease along the southern two tiers of counties in Nebraska\naround midnight, carrying into the early morning hours. Fog \nformation becomes possible once again over northeast Nebraska \nwhere limited mixing keeps dewpoints higher. Efficient cooling \nmay be somewhat limited by cloud debris being wafted in from the\nwest. With both of those factors in mind, it seems prudent to \nkeep patchy fog forecasted, but widespread fog would seem \nsomewhat difficult to develop. Primary time for its development \nwould be 5 AM, lasting until 9 AM. \n\nAnother warm day is forecast for Monday, with early morning \nfog/lingering low clouds limiting potential for that heat and any \nthunderstorms that may develop as well. Heat indices are not \nforecasted to be as high (95-102) as extra mid- and upper-level \nclouds continue to waft overhead.  Showers and storms have a better \nshot at getting off the starting line tomorrow afternoon as strong \nheating and modest convergence occurs in western Nebraska and South \nDakota. Stronger flow aloft will help increase shear values and give \nstorms a better shot at holding together, but that will all depend \non them forming in the first place (20-25% chance). Better chances \nfor the region develop with the LLJ ramping up after sunset but as \nof now, it overshoots the are to ignite storms in South Dakota \ninstead.\n\nTuesday and Beyond:\n\nPushing into the work week, we are set to have one more warm day on \nTuesday before we see temps tumble heading into the latter part of \nthe week. The mid/upper pattern will transition from the semi-\npersistent but weak ridge into a more zonal pattern with the main \njet stream shifting south to be just north of the area. This will \nallow for greater access to increased shear, forcing for ascent via \nembedded shortwaves, and cooler temperatures. We'll see comfortable \ntemperatures in the lower 80s and even 70s at times with nearly \ndaily rain/storm chances, with timing/intensity tough to nail down \nat this point.",
